This is not a question about geomorph or even about geometric morphometrics except to the extent that the theory of shape is relevant for judging a method.  From that perspective, any method that requires you to pick a particular rotation of the data (such as principal components), and whose results depend on that choice, falls into the category of "most worst". But rotation-invariant methods might suffer from other problems, such as assuming a wildly unrealistic model for the evolution of shape.

